      pages: "Pages 2–6, 9–11",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Some applications must process data in strict **chronological order** — either in the order the objects arrived, or in the reverse of that order. A simple list structure is the natural fit for both." },
        { type: "p", text: "A **list** is a finite, ordered sequence of data items called **elements**. Each element has a **position**: first element, second element, and so on. The notation is `<a₀, a₁, …, aₙ₋₁>`." },
        { type: "callout", tone: "source", title: "“Ordered” means positions, not values (p. 3)", text: "A list is ordered because its positions are ordered. The values themselves need not be in ascending order. An unsorted list is still an ordered sequence: element 0 comes before element 1." },
        { type: "p", text: "The **empty list** contains no elements. The **length** is the number of elements currently stored. The beginning is the **head**; the end is the **tail**." },
        { type: "table", caption: "Two kinds of list (p. 5)", head: ["Kind", "Relationship between position and value"], rows: [
          ["Sorted list", "Elements are positioned in ascending order of value."],
          ["Unsorted list", "There is no necessary relationship between element values and positions."]
        ] },
        { type: "h3", text: "A current position" },
        { type: "p", text: "The implementation in this chapter supports a **current position** in addition to the head and tail. The basic operations are: add, find, and delete an element anywhere; move to the next or previous element; clear (reinitialize) the list; and test whether it is empty." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Static versus dynamic data structures (p. 7–8)" },
        { type: "p", text: "The slides distinguish two broad families of data structures by their memory allocation. **Static data structures** are defined and allocated before execution, so their size cannot be changed during execution; the slide’s example is the **array-based list**. **Dynamic data structures** can grow and shrink in size during execution, or permit discarding unwanted memory — the slide’s example is the **linked list** implementation." },
        { type: "h3", text: "The List ADT as an abstract class" },
        { type: "p", text: "The slides define the List ADT with C++ **abstract-class** notation. An abstract class does not specify how its operations are implemented, and it cannot be instantiated. Every concrete list implementation must provide the operations." },
        { type: "code", title: "The List ADT contract (source pp. 9–10, cleaned to standard C++ syntax)", language: "cpp", code: String.raw`// datatype stands for the element type chosen by the application.
class List {
public:
    virtual ~List() = default;

    virtual void clear() = 0;                 // remove all objects
    virtual bool insert(datatype item) = 0;    // insert at current position
    virtual bool append(datatype item) = 0;    // insert at the tail
    virtual bool remove() = 0;                // remove current element
    virtual void setStart() = 0;              // set position to first
    virtual void setEnd() = 0;                // set position to last
    virtual void prev() = 0;                  // move to previous position
    virtual void next() = 0;                  // move to next position
    virtual int length() = 0;                 // number of elements
    virtual bool setPos(int pos) = 0;         // set current position
    virtual bool getValue(datatype& out) = 0; // return current value
    virtual void print() = 0;                 // print all elements
};` },
        { type: "p", text: "The source writes `getValue()` with no parameter and assumes an output variable; the version above passes the result through a reference so the return value can report success or failure. That is a small interface cleanup, not a change in the ADT." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Insert is relative to the current position" },
        { type: "p", text: "Suppose the list is `(12, 32, 15)` and the current element is `32`. Calling `insert(99)` places the new item **before** the current element, giving `(12, 99, 32, 15)`." },
        { type: "table", caption: "The source example (p. 11)", head: ["Operation", "Current element", "Resulting list"], rows: [
          ["Start", "32", "`(12, 32, 15)`"],
          ["`insert(99)`", "32", "`(12, 99, 32, 15)`"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "The deck also sketches a traversal loop. Its `length() > 0` condition is schematic, not a correct termination test: the list length does not change as the current position advances. A real traversal must stop when the position reaches the length." },
        { type: "callout", tone: "info", title: "One ADT, several implementations (p. 12)", text: "The slides introduce two standard implementations: the **array-based list** and the **linked list**. Different applications use the operations with different frequencies, so more than one implementation is justified." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "virtual-functions",
