      pages: "Pages 7–8",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "The List ADT is written with `virtual` functions. A **virtual function** lets a base-class pointer or reference call the version defined by the object’s actual (dynamic) type, not the version chosen from the pointer’s declared type." },
        { type: "p", text: "In the source example, `Drink` declares `virtual void pour()`, and `milk` overrides it. The pointer `D1` has declared type `Drink*` but points at a `milk` object, so `D1->pour()` prints “Milk poured.”" },
        { type: "code", title: "Compile-and-run check", language: "cpp", verified: "MSVC 19.44 · /std:c++17 /EHsc", code: String.raw`#include <iostream>

class Drink {
public:
    virtual ~Drink() = default;
    virtual void pour() const {          // virtual: has a body
        std::cout << "Drink poured.\n";
    }
};

class Milk : public Drink {
public:
    void pour() const override {         // overrides the base version
        std::cout << "Milk poured.\n";
    }
};

// Pure virtual: no body, so Vessel cannot be instantiated.
class Vessel {
public:
    virtual ~Vessel() = default;
    virtual void fill() const = 0;
};

class Cup : public Vessel {
public:
    void fill() const override {
        std::cout << "Cup filled.\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Drink drink;
    Milk milk;

    Drink* d1 = &drink;                  // static type Drink, dynamic type Drink
    Drink* d2 = &milk;                   // static type Drink, dynamic type Milk
    d1->pour();
    d2->pour();

    // Vessel v;                         // error: abstract class
    Cup cup;
    Vessel* v = &cup;
    v->fill();
}` },
        { type: "code", title: "Actual output", language: "text", code: String.raw`Drink poured.
Milk poured.
Cup filled.` },
        { type: "p", text: "Replacing the body of `pour()` with `= 0` makes it a **pure virtual function**. The class then has at least one unimplemented operation, so it is **abstract**: it cannot be instantiated, and every derived concrete class must override the pure virtual function." },
        { type: "callout", tone: "source", title: "Why the ADT is abstract (p. 8)", text: "`virtual void pour() = 0;` states that every drink can be poured, but the base class does not say how. This is exactly the List ADT’s situation: the contract lists `insert`, `remove`, and the other operations, while each implementation supplies the bodies." },
        { type: "note", text: "The source examples do not declare destructors. The compiled versions add `virtual ~Drink()` and `virtual ~Vessel()` because deleting a derived object through a base pointer without a virtual destructor is undefined behavior. This is standard C++ practice, not a claim from the slide." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "array-list",
      title: "Array-based list implementation",
      pages: "Pages 12–16",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "An **array-based list** stores its elements in contiguous memory and tracks the maximum capacity, the current number of elements, and the current position. It is the first of the two standard implementations." },
        { type: "p", text: "Inserting at the head requires shifting every existing element one position toward the tail to open a gap. That shift is the defining cost of the array representation: **Θ(n)** in the worst case." },
        { type: "diagram", title: "Insert at the head shifts the whole array", text: String.raw`before:  [ 12 | 32 | 15 |    |    ]   listSize = 3
insert 99 at position 0
after:   [ 99 | 12 | 32 | 15 |    ]   listSize = 4
                 ^    ^    ^
                 each existing element moved one slot right` },
        { type: "p", text: "The class keeps four data members: `maxSize`, `listSize`, `curr`, and a pointer to the dynamically allocated `listArray`. The constructor allocates the array; the destructor releases it; `clear()` deletes and reallocates the array and resets the counters." },
        { type: "code", title: "Array-based list — cleaned-up, compiled C++17 version of the slide class", language: "cpp", verified: "MSVC 19.44 · /std:c++17 /EHsc", code: String.raw`#include <iostream>

class AList {
public:
    explicit AList(int maxSize)
        : maxSize(maxSize), listSize(0), curr(0),
          listArray(new int[maxSize]) {}

    ~AList() { delete[] listArray; }

    AList(const AList&) = delete;
    AList& operator=(const AList&) = delete;

    void clear() {
        delete[] listArray;
        listArray = new int[maxSize];
        listSize = curr = 0;
    }

    void setStart() { curr = 0; }
    void setEnd() { curr = listSize; }
    void prev() { if (curr > 0) --curr; }
    void next() { if (curr < listSize) ++curr; }
    int length() const { return listSize; }

    bool setPos(int pos) {
        if (pos >= 0 && pos <= listSize) {
            curr = pos;
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    bool getValue(int& out) const {
        if (curr < 0 || curr >= listSize) return false;
        out = listArray[curr];
        return true;
    }

    bool insert(int item) {
        if (listSize == maxSize) return false;
        for (int i = listSize; i > curr; --i) {
            listArray[i] = listArray[i - 1];   // shift toward the tail
        }
        listArray[curr] = item;
        ++listSize;
        return true;
    }

    bool append(int item) {
        if (listSize == maxSize) return false;
        listArray[listSize++] = item;
        return true;
    }

    bool remove() {
        if (curr < 0 || curr >= listSize) return false;
        for (int i = curr; i < listSize - 1; ++i) {
            listArray[i] = listArray[i + 1];   // close the gap
        }
        --listSize;
        return true;
    }

    void print() const {
        std::cout << '(';
        for (int i = 0; i < listSize; ++i) {
            if (i) std::cout << ", ";
            std::cout << listArray[i];
        }
        std::cout << ")\n";
    }

private:
    int maxSize;
    int listSize;
    int curr;
    int* listArray;
};

int main() {
    AList list(8);
    list.append(12);
    list.append(32);
    list.append(15);
    std::cout << "after appends:   ";
    list.print();

    list.setPos(1);          // current element is 32
    list.insert(99);         // insert before the current element
    std::cout << "after insert 99: ";
    list.print();

    list.setPos(0);
    list.remove();
    std::cout << "after remove:    ";
    list.print();
}` },
        { type: "code", title: "Actual output", language: "text", code: String.raw`after appends:   (12, 32, 15)
after insert 99: (12, 99, 32, 15)
after remove:    (99, 32, 15)` },
        { type: "table", caption: "Array-based list costs", head: ["Operation", "Cost", "Reason"], rows: [
          ["Index / access at position k", "Θ(1)", "Address arithmetic reaches the cell directly"],
          ["Append at the tail", "Θ(1)", "Write at `listSize` if capacity remains"],
          ["Insert at the head", "Θ(n)", "Shift all n existing elements one slot right"],
          ["Insert at the current position", "O(n)", "Shift the elements after the gap"],
          ["Remove at the current position", "O(n)", "Shift the elements after the gap left"],
          ["Clear", "Θ(n) to delete, Θ(maxSize) to reallocate", "The backing array is replaced"]
        ] },
        { type: "callout", tone: "warn", title: "Capacity is a hard limit", text: "`insert` and `append` return `false` when `listSize == maxSize`. Growing the list requires allocating a larger array and copying every element — another Θ(n) operation. A linked list avoids this particular limit." },
        { type: "note", text: "Cleanups from the slide code: `datatype` becomes `int`; `getValue` returns through a reference; `next()` tests `curr < listSize` rather than `curr <= listSize`; and the copy constructor is disabled so two lists cannot double-delete the same array. The insert/remove loops match the slide exactly." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "linked-nodes",
      title: "Linked lists: nodes, pointers, and dynamic memory",
      pages: "Pages 17–25",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "The second implementation uses **pointers** and is called a **linked list**. Instead of one contiguous block, it allocates memory for each new element as needed — **dynamic memory allocation** — and connects the elements with links." },
        { type: "p", text: "A linked list is a series of objects called **nodes**. Each node has two parts: a **data part** holding the element, and a **link part** holding the address of another node. The link is a pointer." },
        { type: "diagram", title: "A singly linked list (source p. 22)", text: String.raw`start
  |
  v
+------+------+    +------+------+    +------+------+    +------+------+
|  a   | next | -> |  b   | next | -> |  c   | next | -> |  d   | NULL |
+------+------+    +------+------+    +------+------+    +------+------+
 data   link        data   link        data   link        data   link` },
        { type: "p", text: "Because a list node is a distinct object rather than a cell in an array, the slides recommend making it a separate structure or class. A **structure** groups variables of different types under one name. A **pointer** stores the memory address of another variable or object and is declared as `datatype* name;`." },
        { type: "code", title: "The node type and the header pointer (source pp. 24–25)", language: "cpp", code: String.raw`struct Node {
    int data;        // the element
    Node* next;      // address of the next node
};

Node* start = nullptr;   // points at the first node; null means empty` },
        { type: "p", text: "The **header pointer** `start` points at the first node. In a **singly linked list (SLL)**, each node has exactly one link, containing the address of the next node. The last node’s link contains `NULL` (`nullptr` in modern C++), which marks the end of the list." },
        { type: "table", caption: "The two basic linked-list types (pp. 20–21)", head: ["Type", "Links per node", "Traversal"], rows: [
          ["Singly linked list", "One: next", "Forward only"],
          ["Doubly linked list", "Two: next and previous", "Forward and backward"]
        ] },
        { type: "callout", tone: "info", title: "Nodes are not array cells", text: "An array cell is reached by arithmetic from the array’s base address. A node is reached only by following a pointer from another node (or from `start`). That is why a linked list gives up O(1) indexing but gains O(1) insertion and deletion once the relevant position is known." },
        { type: "p", text: "The basic operations on a list are creating it, inserting an element, deleting an element, and searching for an element. The next two sections cover the three insertion cases and the three deletion cases for a singly linked list." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "sll-insert",
      title: "Singly linked list: the three insertion cases",
      pages: "Pages 26–33",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Insertion into a singly linked list has three cases: **at the beginning**, **at the end**, and **after a particular node**. The cases differ in how many links must be changed and how far the code must walk to find the insertion point." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Case 1 — insertion at the beginning" },
        { type: "p", text: "Two steps are required: make the new node’s `next` point at the current first node, then make `start` point at the new node. The order matters. If `start` is overwritten first, the address of the old first node is lost." },
        { type: "diagram", title: "Insert at the beginning", text: String.raw`before:  start -> [ a | • ] -> [ b | NULL ]

             +--------------+
             |              v
after:   start -> [ x | • ]    [ a | • ] -> [ b | NULL ]` },
        { type: "p", text: "If the list is empty (`start == NULL`), there is no old first node. Simply make `start` point at the new node." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Case 2 — insertion at the end" },
        { type: "p", text: "Start at the first node and follow `next` until the current node’s `next` is `NULL`. That node is the last node. Set its `next` to the new node. The new node’s `next` is already `NULL`." },
        { type: "p", text: "Because the code must walk the entire list, insertion at the end is **O(n)** when only the head pointer is stored. If the list also stores a **tail pointer**, the same operation becomes **O(1)**." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Case 3 — insertion after a particular node" },
        { type: "p", text: "Locate the node `q` after which the new node `p` should go, then perform two link changes: make `p->next` point at `q->next`, then make `q->next` point at `p`. Again, the first assignment must happen before the second, or the rest of the list becomes unreachable." },
        { type: "code", title: "Complete insertion-only SLL — compiled C++17 version of the slide functions", language: "cpp", verified: "MSVC 19.44 · /std:c++17 /EHsc", code: String.raw`#include <iostream>

struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

class SinglyLinkedList {
public:
    SinglyLinkedList() : head(nullptr) {}

    ~SinglyLinkedList() {
        while (head != nullptr) {
            Node* victim = head;
            head = head->next;
            delete victim;
        }
    }

    SinglyLinkedList(const SinglyLinkedList&) = delete;
    SinglyLinkedList& operator=(const SinglyLinkedList&) = delete;

    // Case 1: insert at the beginning. O(1).
    void insertBeginning(int value) {
        Node* node = new Node{value, head};
        head = node;
    }

    // Case 2: insert at the end. O(n) without a tail pointer.
    void insertEnd(int value) {
        Node* node = new Node{value, nullptr};
        if (head == nullptr) {
            head = node;
            return;
        }
        Node* last = head;
        while (last->next != nullptr) {
            last = last->next;
        }
        last->next = node;
    }

    // Case 3: insert after the node holding 'after'. O(n) to locate it.
    bool insertAfter(int after, int value) {
        Node* q = head;
        while (q != nullptr && q->data != after) {
            q = q->next;
        }
        if (q == nullptr) return false;       // no such node
        Node* node = new Node{value, q->next};
        q->next = node;
        return true;
    }

    void print(const char* label) const {
        std::cout << label << ": ";
        for (Node* p = head; p != nullptr; p = p->next) {
            std::cout << p->data;
            if (p->next != nullptr) std::cout << " -> ";
        }
        std::cout << " -> NULL\n";
    }

private:
    Node* head;
};

int main() {
    SinglyLinkedList list;

    list.insertBeginning(10);
    list.insertBeginning(20);   // 20 -> 10
    list.insertEnd(30);         // 20 -> 10 -> 30
    list.insertAfter(10, 15);   // 20 -> 10 -> 15 -> 30
    list.print("built");
}` },
        { type: "code", title: "Actual output", language: "text", code: String.raw`built: 20 -> 10 -> 15 -> 30 -> NULL` },
        { type: "table", caption: "Insertion costs", head: ["Case", "Cost", "Why"], rows: [
          ["Beginning", "O(1)", "Two pointer assignments; no traversal"],
          ["End, head pointer only", "O(n)", "Walk to the last node"],
          ["End, tail pointer available", "O(1)", "Update tail->next and the tail pointer"],
          ["After a known node", "O(1)", "Two pointer assignments"],
          ["After a value (search included)", "O(n)", "Find the node first"]
        ] },
        { type: "callout", tone: "warn", title: "The classic pointer-order bug", text: "In `insertAfter`, writing `q->next = node` before `node->next = q->next` loses the address of the old successor. The new node then points at itself or at an arbitrary value. Always connect the new node to the rest of the list first." },
        { type: "note", text: "The slides use a global `start` pointer and free functions such as `insert_beg(node* p)`, `insert_end(node* p)`, and `insert_after(int c, node* p)`. The compiled version packages the same logic in a class and allocates nodes with `new`. The source’s “insert after position c” loop and its prints are preserved in spirit; the value-based search above is easier to test." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "sll-delete",
      title: "Singly linked list: the three deletion cases",
      pages: "Pages 34–40",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Deletion also has three cases: **the first node**, **the last node**, and **an intermediate (particular) node**. In every case the node must be unlinked from the list and then released with `delete`; otherwise the memory leaks." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Case 1 — deleting the first node" },
        { type: "p", text: "Save the first node in a temporary pointer, move `start` to the second node (`temp->next`), then `delete temp`. If the list is empty, report an error or return `false`." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Case 2 — deleting the last node" },
        { type: "p", text: "Find the **second-last** node `q` — the node whose `next->next` is `NULL` — set `q->next = NULL`, then delete the old last node. This requires a full traversal, so it is **O(n)**." },
        { type: "callout", tone: "warn", title: "The single-node list is a special case", text: "If the list has exactly one node, there is no second-last node. The test `q->next->next` dereferences a null pointer. A correct implementation checks `head->next == NULL` first and deletes the head directly." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Case 3 — deleting a particular node" },
        { type: "p", text: "Find the node **before** the one to delete, call it `q`. Let `p = q->next` be the victim. Set `q->next = p->next` to bypass `p`, then `delete p`. Deleting the head does not fit this pattern because there is no predecessor, so the head case must be handled separately." },
        { type: "code", title: "Complete SLL deletion program — compiled C++17 version", language: "cpp", verified: "MSVC 19.44 · /std:c++17 /EHsc", code: String.raw`#include <iostream>

struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

class SinglyLinkedList {
public:
    SinglyLinkedList() : head(nullptr) {}

    ~SinglyLinkedList() {
        while (head != nullptr) {
            Node* victim = head;
            head = head->next;
            delete victim;
        }
    }

    SinglyLinkedList(const SinglyLinkedList&) = delete;
    SinglyLinkedList& operator=(const SinglyLinkedList&) = delete;

    void insertEnd(int value) {
        Node* node = new Node{value, nullptr};
        if (head == nullptr) {
            head = node;
            return;
        }
        Node* last = head;
        while (last->next != nullptr) {
            last = last->next;
        }
        last->next = node;
    }

    // Deletion case 1: the first node. O(1).
    bool deleteFirst() {
        if (head == nullptr) return false;
        Node* victim = head;
        head = head->next;
        delete victim;
        return true;
    }

    // Deletion case 2: the last node. O(n).
    bool deleteLast() {
        if (head == nullptr) return false;
        if (head->next == nullptr) {          // single-node list
            delete head;
            head = nullptr;
            return true;
        }
        Node* q = head;
        while (q->next->next != nullptr) {
            q = q->next;
        }
        delete q->next;
        q->next = nullptr;
        return true;
    }

    // Deletion case 3: the node holding 'value'. O(n).
    bool deleteValue(int value) {
        if (head == nullptr) return false;
        if (head->data == value) {            // deleting the head
            Node* victim = head;
            head = head->next;
            delete victim;
            return true;
        }
        Node* q = head;
        while (q->next != nullptr && q->next->data != value) {
            q = q->next;
        }
        if (q->next == nullptr) return false; // value not found
        Node* victim = q->next;
        q->next = victim->next;
        delete victim;
        return true;
    }

    void print(const char* label) const {
        std::cout << label << ": ";
        for (Node* p = head; p != nullptr; p = p->next) {
            std::cout << p->data;
            if (p->next != nullptr) std::cout << " -> ";
        }
        std::cout << " -> NULL\n";
    }

private:
    Node* head;
};

int main() {
    SinglyLinkedList list;
    list.insertEnd(10);
    list.insertEnd(20);
    list.insertEnd(30);
    list.insertEnd(40);
    list.print("built");

    list.deleteFirst();            // remove 10
    list.print("after deleteFirst");
    list.deleteLast();             // remove 40
    list.print("after deleteLast");
    list.deleteValue(20);          // remove the interior node 20
    list.print("after deleteValue(20)");
    list.deleteValue(99);          // not present: returns false
    list.print("after deleteValue(99)");
}` },
        { type: "code", title: "Actual output", language: "text", code: String.raw`built: 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> 40 -> NULL
after deleteFirst: 20 -> 30 -> 40 -> NULL
after deleteLast: 20 -> 30 -> NULL
after deleteValue(20): 30 -> NULL
after deleteValue(99): 30 -> NULL` },
        { type: "table", caption: "Deletion costs", head: ["Case", "Cost", "Why"], rows: [
          ["First node", "O(1)", "Update `start` and delete"],
          ["Last node", "O(n)", "Find the second-last node"],
          ["Interior node, predecessor known", "O(1)", "Bypass and delete"],
