        ] },
        { type: "callout", tone: "warn", title: "The slide sketches are not edge-case safe", text: "`del_last` in the deck executes `q->next->next` without checking for a one-node list; `del` does not handle deleting the first node, and its `if (q == NULL)` sits inside the traversal loop after the dereference. Those sketches assume a valid interior position. The compiled program above adds the missing checks." },
        { type: "note", text: "The deck’s `del(int c)` deletes by **position**, using a loop to find the predecessor. The compiled example deletes by **value**, which makes the tests readable. Both are O(n) because both must find the node first." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "arrays-vs-linked",
      title: "Arrays versus linked lists",
      pages: "Pages 41–42",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "The choice between an array-based list and a linked list is a choice about which operations matter. The slides compare the two on size flexibility, insertion and deletion, indexing, memory use, and sequential access." },
        { type: "table", caption: "Qualitative comparison (source p. 41)", head: ["Property", "Array", "Linked list"], rows: [
          ["Size", "Fixed size; resizing is expensive", "Dynamic size"],
          ["Insertion / deletion", "Inefficient: elements are usually shifted", "Efficient: no shifting"],
          ["Random access", "Efficient indexing", "No random access; not suitable for index-based operations such as sorting"],
          ["Memory use", "Waste if the array is not full", "Allocated as needed; no unused capacity"],
          ["Sequential access", "Faster, because elements are contiguous", "Slower, because elements are not contiguous"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "The second table makes the trade-off precise for a singly linked list. It is the table to memorize for exams and for design reviews." },
        { type: "table", caption: "Operation complexity: array versus SLL (source p. 42)", head: ["Operation", "Array", "Singly linked list"], rows: [
          ["Insert at beginning", "O(n)", "O(1)"],
          ["Insert at end", "O(1)", "O(1) with a tail reference; O(n) without one"],
          ["Insert at middle", "O(n)", "O(n)"],
          ["Delete at beginning", "O(n)", "O(1)"],
          ["Delete at end", "O(1)", "O(n)"],
          ["Delete at middle", "O(n): O(1) access + O(n) shift", "O(n): O(n) search + O(1) delete"],
          ["Search", "O(n) linear; O(log n) binary if sorted", "O(n)"],
          ["Indexing: element at position k", "O(1)", "O(n)"]
        ] },
        { type: "callout", tone: "info", title: "Read the table as a trade, not a winner", text: "The SLL wins at the front; the array wins at indexing, at the end (without a tail pointer), and on binary search when sorted. There is no universally better structure — only a better fit for a workload." },
        { type: "callout", tone: "warn", title: "Two nuances behind the slide’s wording", text: "First, “no memory waste” refers to unused **capacity**: a linked list allocates only the nodes it needs. Each node still stores a pointer, so the per-element overhead is higher than an array’s. Second, arrays support O(1) indexing, which is why sorting and binary search are practical on arrays and awkward on singly linked lists." },
        { type: "p", text: "This is the same lesson as Chapter 1’s bank example: choose the structure from the operation mix. If the program inserts and deletes at the front constantly, the array’s O(n) shift will dominate. If it reads `a[k]` constantly, the linked list’s O(n) walk will dominate." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "doubly-linked",
      title: "Doubly linked lists",
      pages: "Pages 43–54",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "A **doubly linked list (DLL)** is a linked structure of sequentially linked nodes where each node has **three fields**: the data, a link to the next node, and a link to the previous node. The beginning node’s `previous` link and the ending node’s `next` link point to a terminator — typically `NULL` or a sentinel node." },
        { type: "code", title: "DLL node structure (source p. 46)", language: "cpp", code: String.raw`struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
    Node* previous;   // address of the previous node
};` },
        { type: "diagram", title: "Three fields per node (source p. 44)", text: String.raw`        +----------+------+----------+
NULL <- | previous | data | next     | -> ... -> NULL
        +----------+------+----------+
                     ^
              one DLL node` },
        { type: "table", caption: "DLL compared with SLL (source p. 45)", head: ["Advantages", "Disadvantages"], rows: [
          ["Can be traversed in either direction, which may be essential for some programs.", "Requires more space: one extra pointer per node."],
          ["Deletion and inserting before a node become easier.", "List manipulations are slower, because more links must be changed."],
          ["", "Greater chance of bugs, because more links must be manipulated."]
        ] },
        { type: "h3", text: "Insertion at the beginning" },
        { type: "p", text: "If the list is empty, point `start` at the new node. Otherwise save the old first node in `temp`, make `start` point at the new node, set `temp->previous` to the new node, and set the new node’s `next` to `temp`." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Insertion at the end" },
        { type: "p", text: "If the list is empty, point `start` at the new node. Otherwise walk to the last node `temp`, set `temp->next` to the new node, and set the new node’s `previous` to `temp`." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Insertion after a node" },
        { type: "p", text: "Locate the node `temp` after which the new node `p` will go. Four links are involved: connect `p` to `temp->next`, connect that successor’s `previous` back to `p`, connect `temp->next` to `p`, and set `p->previous` to `temp`. Update the successor first and the predecessor last, so no link is lost." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Deletion" },
        { type: "p", text: "Deleting from a DLL changes **two links**: the predecessor’s `next` must bypass the victim, and the successor’s `previous` must bypass it. Deleting the first or last node is a special case because one of those neighbours is the terminator. The source’s `del_at(int c)` handles a general interior node; a robust implementation must also update `head`/`tail` when the victim is at an end." },
        { type: "code", title: "Complete doubly linked list — compiled C++17 version", language: "cpp", verified: "MSVC 19.44 · /std:c++17 /EHsc", code: String.raw`#include <iostream>

struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
    Node* previous;
};

class DoublyLinkedList {
public:
    DoublyLinkedList() : head(nullptr), tail(nullptr) {}

    ~DoublyLinkedList() {
        while (head != nullptr) {
            Node* victim = head;
            head = head->next;
            delete victim;
        }
    }

    DoublyLinkedList(const DoublyLinkedList&) = delete;
    DoublyLinkedList& operator=(const DoublyLinkedList&) = delete;

    void insertBeginning(int value) {
        Node* node = new Node{value, head, nullptr};
        if (head != nullptr) {
            head->previous = node;
        } else {
            tail = node;
        }
        head = node;
    }

    void insertEnd(int value) {
        Node* node = new Node{value, nullptr, tail};
        if (tail != nullptr) {
            tail->next = node;
        } else {
            head = node;
        }
        tail = node;
    }

    bool insertAfter(int after, int value) {
        Node* q = head;
        while (q != nullptr && q->data != after) {
            q = q->next;
        }
        if (q == nullptr) return false;
        Node* node = new Node{value, q->next, q};
        if (q->next != nullptr) {
            q->next->previous = node;
        } else {
            tail = node;
        }
        q->next = node;
        return true;
    }

    bool deleteValue(int value) {
        Node* q = head;
        while (q != nullptr && q->data != value) {
            q = q->next;
        }
        if (q == nullptr) return false;

        if (q->previous != nullptr) {
            q->previous->next = q->next;
        } else {
            head = q->next;                   // deleting the first node
        }
        if (q->next != nullptr) {
            q->next->previous = q->previous;
        } else {
            tail = q->previous;               // deleting the last node
        }
        delete q;
        return true;
    }

    void printForward(const char* label) const {
        std::cout << label << ": ";
        for (Node* p = head; p != nullptr; p = p->next) {
            std::cout << p->data;
            if (p->next != nullptr) std::cout << " -> ";
        }
        std::cout << " -> NULL\n";
    }

    void printBackward(const char* label) const {
        std::cout << label << ": ";
        for (Node* p = tail; p != nullptr; p = p->previous) {
            std::cout << p->data;
            if (p->previous != nullptr) std::cout << " -> ";
        }
        std::cout << " -> NULL\n";
    }

private:
    Node* head;
    Node* tail;
};

int main() {
    DoublyLinkedList list;

    list.insertEnd(10);
    list.insertEnd(30);
    list.insertBeginning(5);
    list.insertAfter(10, 20);

    list.printForward("forward ");
    list.printBackward("backward");

    list.deleteValue(30);
    list.printForward("after delete 30");
}` },
        { type: "code", title: "Actual output", language: "text", code: String.raw`forward : 5 -> 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> NULL
backward: 30 -> 20 -> 10 -> 5 -> NULL
after delete 30: 5 -> 10 -> 20 -> NULL` },
        { type: "callout", tone: "warn", title: "More links, more bugs", text: "The deck lists a greater chance of bugs as a DLL disadvantage, and the reason is visible in `insertAfter`: two links must be updated in the correct order. A missed `previous` update breaks backward traversal even though forward traversal still looks correct." },
        { type: "note", text: "The slide’s `del_at(int c)` deletes by position and assumes an interior node. It also contains a stray closing brace in the extracted text. The compiled version deletes by value and handles the head and tail cases explicitly." }
      ]
    },
