        { type: "callout", tone: "source", title: "Reading pointer (p. 56)", text: "The deck ends with a reading assignment: **Circular Linked Lists**. The chapter itself does not define or implement circular lists, so this academy does not invent details beyond the pointer." },
        { type: "p", text: "The next structures in the course build directly on this chapter. A **stack** restricts insertion and deletion to one end of a list. A **queue** inserts at one end and deletes at the other. A **tree** replaces the single `next` link with several child links. In every case, the question from Chapter 1 returns: which operations dominate, and what does each layout cost?" },
        { type: "callout", tone: "tip", title: "A one-sentence summary", text: "An array buys O(1) indexing with O(n) shifting; a linked list buys O(1) insertion and deletion at a known position with O(n) search and no random access." }
      ]
