      prompt: "Starting from an empty singly linked list, perform these operations in order: insertBeginning(5), insertEnd(9), insertAfter(5, 7), deleteFirst(), deleteLast(), insertEnd(4), insertAfter(7, 6). Draw the list after each operation, including the NULL terminator.",
      hint: "insertAfter(value, newValue) inserts newValue immediately after the node holding value. deleteLast removes the only node when the list has length 1.",
      solution: "insertBeginning(5):   5 -> NULL\ninsertEnd(9):         5 -> 9 -> NULL\ninsertAfter(5, 7):    5 -> 7 -> 9 -> NULL\ndeleteFirst():        7 -> 9 -> NULL\ndeleteLast():         7 -> NULL\ninsertEnd(4):         7 -> 4 -> NULL\ninsertAfter(7, 6):    7 -> 6 -> 4 -> NULL\n\nAfter the last operation, head points at the node holding 7; that node’s next points at 6; 6’s next points at 4; 4’s next is NULL. Deleting the last node when only 7 remained was the single-node special case: head became NULL before the next insertion."
    },
    {
      id: "ch3-ex-3",
      title: "Write the three SLL insertion functions",
      prompt: "Using `struct Node { int data; Node* next; };` and a `Node* head` member, write `insertBeginning`, `insertEnd`, and `insertAfter` for a singly linked list. Handle the empty list and report failure when the “after” value is absent.",
      hint: "For insertAfter, find the node q with q->data == after, then connect the new node to q->next before changing q->next.",
      solution: "void insertBeginning(int value) {\n    Node* node = new Node{value, head};\n    head = node;\n}\n\nvoid insertEnd(int value) {\n    Node* node = new Node{value, nullptr};\n    if (head == nullptr) { head = node; return; }\n    Node* last = head;\n    while (last->next != nullptr) last = last->next;\n    last->next = node;\n}\n\nbool insertAfter(int after, int value) {\n    Node* q = head;\n    while (q != nullptr && q->data != after) q = q->next;\n    if (q == nullptr) return false;          // value not present\n    Node* node = new Node{value, q->next};\n    q->next = node;\n    return true;\n}\n\ninsertBeginning is O(1). insertEnd is O(n) without a tail pointer. insertAfter is O(n) to find the node, then O(1) to link. In insertAfter, initializing node with q->next before assigning q->next = node preserves the rest of the list."
    },
    {
      id: "ch3-ex-4",
      title: "Make deleteLast correct for every list length",
      prompt: "The slide’s `del_last` advances while `q->next->next != NULL`, then deletes `q->next`. Explain what goes wrong for an empty list and for a one-node list, and write a correct `deleteLast` that returns false when the list is empty.",
      hint: "Check head == nullptr first, then check head->next == nullptr. Only after those checks is it safe to look at q->next->next.",
      solution: "Empty list: head is NULL, so any dereference of head or q is invalid. The function must return false immediately.\n\nOne-node list: head is not NULL, but head->next is NULL. The test q->next->next is therefore NULL->next, which dereferences a null pointer. The correct action is to delete head and set head = NULL.\n\nbool deleteLast() {\n    if (head == nullptr) return false;\n    if (head->next == nullptr) {      // exactly one node\n        delete head;\n        head = nullptr;\n        return true;\n    }\n    Node* q = head;\n    while (q->next->next != nullptr) {\n        q = q->next;\n    }\n    delete q->next;                   // the old last node\n    q->next = nullptr;                // q is now the last node\n    return true;\n}\n\nThe general case is O(n) because it walks to the second-last node. Testing the two boundary cases before the loop removes the null dereferences."
    },
    {
      id: "ch3-ex-5",
      title: "Choose a structure from the workload",
      prompt: "For each workload, choose an array-based list or a singly linked list and justify the choice with the complexity table: (a) insert and delete at the front thousands of times, rare indexing; (b) read a[k] for random k constantly; (c) delete the last element repeatedly; (d) insert at the end repeatedly when a tail pointer is available; (e) search sorted data repeatedly.",
      hint: "Compare the dominant operation, not every operation. Remember that a SLL has no random access and no binary search.",
      solution: "(a) Singly linked list. Insert and delete at the beginning are O(1); the array pays O(n) to shift every element. Rare indexing means the SLL’s O(n) index cost rarely matters.\n\n(b) Array-based list. Indexing is O(1); a SLL must walk k links, which is O(n) per read.\n\n(c) Array-based list. Delete at end is O(1) after decrementing listSize; a SLL must find the second-last node in O(n). A doubly linked list with a tail pointer would also give O(1), but that is not the SLL in the table.\n\n(d) Either, if the array still has capacity. Insert at end is O(1) for both when a tail pointer exists. The array may need an expensive resize when full; the SLL allocates one node per insertion but pays a pointer per element.\n\n(e) Array-based list. Binary search is O(log n) on a sorted array; a SLL has no random access, so search is O(n). Sorting the SLL is also awkward for the same reason.\n\nThe general rule: choose for the dominant operation and accept the weaker costs elsewhere."
    },
    {
      id: "ch3-ex-6",
      title: "Track student records with linked-list operations",
      prompt: "The teacher’s final exercise (source p. 64) asks for a C++ program that keeps track of student records with a single or double linked list. Each student record has a Name, an ID, an Age, and a Department. The program must support: (a) add a new student from the keyboard at the front, middle, or last position; (b) delete a node — first, last, or middle; (c) display the whole list of students; (d) search for a specific node.",
      hint: "Use the three insertion cases (front / end / after a node) and the three deletion cases (first / last / interior) that were just taught. For deletion, unlink before delete to avoid leaks; for a DLL, update both the next and the previous links.",
      solution: "Design: each node holds {name, id, age, department} plus links to next and (for the DLL) previous.\n\nAdd at front: create the node, node->next = head (or DLL: set head->previous = node), head = node.\nAdd at end: walk to the last node and link. Add in the middle: find the position, then insert after the predecessor.\n\nDelete first: move head to head->next (SLL) / fix head and second->previous (DLL), then delete the old head. Delete last: walk to the second-last node and set its next to NULL, then delete the old last node. Delete an interior node: unlink the predecessor’s next to point at the successor and delete the victim.\n\nDisplay: walk from the head with a temporary pointer and print each student’s name, ID, age, and department; stop at NULL (or the sentinel).\n\nSearch: walk the list comparing the ID, returning the node when found and an empty result otherwise.\n\nFor the DLL the same operations are available backward, which makes “search by predecessor” and “delete before a node” convenient; for the SLL the teacher’s insert/delete cases are the authoritative ones.\n\nThe exercise is intentionally open: the teacher does not prescribe the record fields beyond Name / ID / Age / Department, and the student is expected to choose positions and handling for missing values."
    }
