    "SLL deletion has three cases: first (O(1)), last (O(n)), and interior (O(n) to find the predecessor, O(1) to unlink).",
    "Arrays win on indexing, end deletion, and binary search; SLLs win on insertion and deletion at the front.",
    "A doubly linked list adds a previous pointer, enabling backward traversal and easier deletion at the cost of space and more link updates.",
    "Linked lists model MRU lists, browser history, undo stacks, and the nodes of stacks, hash tables, and trees."
  ],
  keyTerms: [
    { term: "List", definition: "A finite, ordered sequence of data items called elements." },
    { term: "Current position", definition: "The element that insert, remove, and getValue operate on." },
    { term: "List ADT", definition: "The abstract contract of list values and operations, independent of implementation." },
    { term: "Virtual function", definition: "A base-class function whose derived override is chosen from the object’s dynamic type." },
    { term: "Pure virtual function", definition: "A virtual function declared `= 0`; it makes the class abstract." },
    { term: "Node", definition: "A dynamically allocated object holding data and one or more links." },
    { term: "Header pointer", definition: "The pointer (often called start or head) that points at the first node." },
